<content>
  <section><title> Different Exponential Representations</title>
<para>A table of values is shown below for a function \(g(x)=(\frac{1}{2})^x\), which represents exponential decay.</para>
<table>
  <tgroup cols="8"><thead>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(x\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>–3</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>–2</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>–1</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>0</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>1</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>2</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>3</para>
      </entry>
	  </row></thead><tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(g(x) = (\frac{1}{2})^x\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>8</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>4</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>2</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>1</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(\frac12\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(\frac14\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(\frac18\)</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</tgroup></table>
<para>When these points are graphed, they show the decay because the function graphed decreases from left to right:</para>
<media><image alt="" role="presentation" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/640dc842516ed96046308a6b80d6a2df0ebdf749"/></media><title> Try It: Different Exponential Representations</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Make a table for the function \(f(x)=4^x\). Then graph the function.</para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>Here is how to make a table and then graph the exponential function:</para>
  <para>First, substitute values in for \(x\) to find the values of \(f(x)\).</para>
  <table>
    
    <tgroup cols="6"><thead>
      <row>
        <entry>
          <para>\(x\)</para>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <para>0</para>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <para>1</para>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <para>2</para>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <para>3</para>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <para>4</para>
        </entry>
		</row></thead><tbody>
      <row>
        <entry>
          <para> \(f(x)=4^x\)</para>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <para>\(4^0=1\)</para>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <para>\(4^1=4\)</para>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <para>\(4^2=16\)</para>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <para>\(4^3=64\)</para>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <para>\(4^4=256\)</para>
        </entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
  </tgroup></table>
  <media><image alt="" role="presentation" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c4e972074be9c587e82641a2054c2fead8f5a718"/></media></solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
